\caption(Explicit 4-epoch Adam fit and a tensor-computed true/predicted-class count matrix at the selected checkpoint. Training uses augmentation and batch BN moments; validation/test use saved moving moments. Counts sum to 64; IDs follow the original CIFAR-10 order.)
\alttext(Explicit 4-epoch Adam fit and a tensor-computed true/predicted-class count matrix at the selected checkpoint. Training uses augmentation and batch BN moments; validation/test use saved moving moments. Counts sum to 64; IDs follow the original CIFAR-10 order.)


> Observed subset result

Validation selected epoch 1: validation NLL 2.536862, inference training NLL
2.413752 and test NLL 2.549119; 2/64 test predictions are correct (3.125%).
After only 64 updates the 5.9-million-parameter model has not left chance level
(NLL 2.30 for ten classes), so this short run shows the explicit optimizer and
checkpoint selection at work, not a trained classifier. No speed or progressive-training gain is measured.

> Native confusion matrices

These matrices use the held-out predictions from the executed program above.
The native `ConfusionMatrix` SVG API labels exact counts with true classes
running from top to bottom and predicted classes from left to right.

\image(figures/efficientnet_v2_confusion_api_0.svg)
\caption(Held-out classification confusion counts, evaluation 1. Exact cell counts are printed in the image.)
\alttext(Integer prediction counts with true classes as rows and predicted classes as columns.)


> Conclusion

Fused early filtering changes learned computation and hardware arithmetic. Full table counts,
precise target geometry and visible base-B0 fitting explain that design without treating fused
blocks as equivalent functions or claiming the published progressive-training results.

> References

- [Tan and Le (2021), EfficientNetV2: Smaller Models and Faster Training](https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.00298).
- [Google EfficientNetV2 release](https://github.com/google/automl/tree/master/efficientnetv2).
- [CIFAR-10 dataset](https://www.cs.toronto.edu/~kriz/cifar.html).
